<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Abydos</h1><p class="st-meta">680 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Abydos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Milesian colony at the narrowest point of the Hellespont, where Xerxes bridged the strait and where legend placed the love of Hero and Leander.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> founded Abydos c. 680 BCE on the Asian shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, at the narrows opposite <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sestos">Sestos</a>, with the agreement of the Lydian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges</a>. Its position controlled the crossing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a> and the passage of ships to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Abydos lived from trade and fishing. In 480 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> built his boat bridges across the strait near the city. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> but revolted in 411 BCE, becoming a Spartan base, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-abydos">Battle of Abydos</a> was fought off its shore that year. Greek legend set the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-and-leander">Leander</a>, who swam to Hero at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sestos">Sestos</a>, here.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 200 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia</a> besieged Abydos; rather than surrender, many citizens killed their families and themselves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> later freed it, and it joined the province of Asia in 129 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 680 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:46.17%;width:10.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>680 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Abydos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abydus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=abydos" aria-label="Abydos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5329 740 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Abydos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5649 996L6140 1251M5649 996L5591 1068M5649 996L5552 978"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5552 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5649 996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5649 996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Abydos. <a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=abydos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

